3. Longest Repeating Char Replacement:

Code:
class Solution {
    public int characterReplacement(String s, int k) {
        int[] count = new int[26];

        int left = 0;
        int maxFrequency = 0;
        int maxLength = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {

            // Add current character
            int index = s.charAt(right) - 'A';
            count[index]++;

            // Highest frequency in current/previous window
            maxFrequency = Math.max(maxFrequency, count[index]);

            // If replacements required > k, shrink window
            while ((right - left + 1) - maxFrequency > k) {
                count[s.charAt(left) - 'A']--;
                left++;
            }

            // Current valid window
            maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, right - left + 1);
        }

        return maxLength;
    }
}

Approach:
Use the sliding window technique with a frequency array to track character counts. Keep the window valid when window length - maxFrequency <= k, meaning the remaining characters can be replaced within k operations; otherwise, move left forward.

Complexity:
Time: O(n) — each character is added and removed at most once.
Space: O(1) — the frequency array has a fixed size of 26.

Optimal Code:
class Solution {
    public int characterReplacement(String s, int k) {
        int j=0;
        int i=0;
        int maxC=0;
        int max=0;
        int count[]=new int[26];
        while(j<s.length())
        {
            char c=s.charAt(j);
            count[c-'A']=count[c-'A']+1;
            maxC=Math.max(maxC,count[c-'A']);

            while((j-i+1)-maxC>k)
            { 
                char d=s.charAt(i);
                count[d-'A']=count[d-'A']-1;
                 i++;
                
            }
            max=Math.max(max,(j-i+1));
            j++;

        }
        return max;
    }
}

Approach:
Use the sliding window technique with a frequency array to track character counts and maxC for the most frequent character in the window. If the number of replacements needed exceeds k, move i forward to shrink the window; otherwise, update the maximum window length.

Complexity:
Time: O(n) — each character is processed at most twice (added and removed).
Space: O(1) — the frequency array has a fixed size of 26.

